John Clark in #93-10-508 posted:

    I'm a little worried.  I hope the recent criticism of Phillip
    Zimmermann is not the start of a McCarthy style witch-hint to
    ferret out those who engage in un-Extropian activities.
    Mr.Zimmermann, while not ideologically pure and whatever his
    personal beliefs has nevertheless done more to advance the ideas
    we value than most of us on this list.  Those who protest
    Mr.Zimmermann's actions should demand that he return all the money
    you paid him for PGP.  Perhaps I'm overreacting but I never want
    to read a post that starts "I am holding in my hand a paper with
    the names of 14 members of the Extropy Institute who are card
    carrying members of pro-state organizations".

As one of those who started this thread, I feel I should respond.

First, it's incorrect to compare the abuse of Congressional
(government) power typified by the McCarthy hearings to the
dissimination of public information and personal experience, which is
what I did.  The latter is completely EC and LC, IMHO, and protected
by the 1st amendment.

As Tim May made clear with his "BlackNet Dossier Service", if you
don't want your public utterances and postings coming back to haunt
you, better protect yourself with the tools of strong cryptography and
remailers.  Welcome to the information age; get used to it.

Second, I stated in my first message on this subject that despite his
personal flaws, I regard PRZ as an Extropian hero for his
contributions to and sacrifices for PGP. I would not discourage anyone
with spare cash from contributing to his defense fund.  I will
continue, if allowed, to contribute my modest programming efforts to
the PGP development group without any expectation of direct
remuneration.

    PS:  Does anyone understand why the nuclear freeze movement and
    Carl Sagan are supposed to be our enemies, because I don't.

The "nuclear freeze" movement (now deservedly defunct) was our enemy
for the same reason advocates of gun control are our enemies. Both
seek/sought to limit our legitimate needs for self-defense.

Until the day "limited government" withers away to anarcho-capitalism,
one of the only legitimate functions of government, specified in the
Constitution, is to "provide for the common defense."

In hindsight, at least, it should be OTTMI (obvious to the meanest
intelligence) that the "arms race" and MAD was a successful policy.
The cold war is over; we won!  Without firing a shot!  (I don't call
Korea & Vietnam part of the cold war).

As for Carl Sagan; although he's not as extreme as some, he's
definitely part of the Apocalyptic Environmentalist movement, which
seeks to use speculative "science" (like "nuclear winter" and
"greenhouse gas" and "ozone hole") hysteria to limit the application
of technology.  The same camp as Jeremy Rifkin, who never saw a new
technology he liked.  Surely everyone on this list can see that this
conflicts with the core Extropian beliefs of Boundless Expansion and
Dynamic Optimism.

