Extropians,

I'm sure Duncan will speak for himself, but a few comments about
the post by James A. Donald are in order.  He wrote:

    The US government and the US courts have been ignoring
    the law on many matters for some considerable time.

Yes, but this is irrelevant to the question of offshore bank
accounts.  The government cannot abuse the law regarding your
rights if they don't know you have an offshore account.

James also wrote:

    If you report a foreign bank account you get an
    automatic hostile audit . . . They do not take
    everything you own, they just create enough aggravation
    that //even the very rich and entirely legal, the sort
    of people who have a permanent team of accountants and
    lawyers//, are reluctant to report a foreign bank
    account.  [Emphasis added.]

This is a variation of the baseball player/movie star fallacy.
Every year, the IRS audits some high-profile individuals (Leona
Helmsley comes to mind).  This is done to cow the general public.
("Gee, if they can get a fat cat like her, think what they could
do to a little guy like me.")

The truth is quite the opposite.  Baseball players, movie stars
and captains of industry are sitting ducks.  They are owned by
their property and their success.  It is precisely the "little
guys"--in part because there are so many of them--who are the
hard ones to get.

The IRS is not in the "law enforcement" business; it is not in
the "fairness" business; it is in the "revenue collecting"
business.  Some little plumber who skims cash payments is not
nearly as likely to be an IRS target as is a Donald Trump who
makes a slight technical error in his return.

