Subject: PPL: Custody

Me>> Could anybody explain me how the idea of custody would work under PPL?
 
  I don't mean to sound too critical, but I still don't understand how
it can work...
 
 
Tim Starr:
 
>You would contract into the system you think best for your child, and she
>would contract into one she thought best for herself as soon as she could.
 
Hmm... Suppose my child is smart enough to earn a lot of money and contract
the best agency to protect her 'right' to eat more than 10 chocolate bars a
day that I allow her - she just loves chocolate, and can't control herself;
for a good price she will sure find 'better' custodians who will let he do
it.  Or an uncontrollable craving for chocolate is by itself a sign of
incompetence? - than what do we do with adults who have it?
 
>I like to think of it in terms of presumption of competence.  
 
  Usually, though, they suggest presumption of incompetence until the age
of 18, and presumption of competence after 18.  This has lots of flaws.
First, competence in *what*?   I would presume everybody after the age
of 5 competent enough to handle a fork, and still I'd presume everybody
incompetent to handle a nuclear reactor, until proven otherwise.
I can hardly believe that the competence to drive, drink, smoke, work at
MacDonalds, work at Microsoft, read alt.sex.bestiality, live independently,
bear arms, rear children, etc., etc., comes at about the same age.
18 ?   Who is going to choose this age?  Parents?  How come people who
just biologically produced a child's body (which they may deeply regret)
get to determine the child's future for so many years?  Can I declare
that I presume my children incompetent until they are 60yo?
What about orphans?
 
 
>I presume every-one competent to direct themselves until proven otherwise.  
 
 In general, I agree with this suggestion. But still, I have lots of
questions here:
   Would you give a driving licence to everybody who hasn't proven unable
to drive?
 What about having babies? Are 13-year-olds allowed to try?
If they can't handle the baby, when can they try next time? at 14 ?
Who determines that?
Who takes care of abandoned babies/orphans?
Some of them nobody may be interested in adopting. Will they die?
Otherwise, whose money will be used to bring them up?
 
  Also, who will enforce the restrictions?
(Bear in mind that different agencies may have different views on
the person's competence).
 
 
